# Bounded root reads: one hung root costs one corpus, not the engine [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]

- status: draft
- x-created: 2026-11-05
- scope: No call, boot or poll waits without limit on the disk behind a registered root. A root that does not answer in time is marked, the corpora that need it say so, and every other corpus keeps serving reads and writes.
- boundary: The engine cannot cancel a call stuck in the operating system. It can stop waiting for it, stop issuing more, and say so. A thread stuck in such a call is abandoned, not ended, and goes when the call returns or the process exits.
- not-this: Not a retry scheme, not a health service, and no change to what a corpus holds. Not a promise about a file system that lies about its state: a read that returns wrong bytes promptly is out of reach.
- rationale: Measured on one deployment with a stall model, a test that blocks stat, listing and open under one of two roots, at release 19d6511: named reads of the healthy corpus blocked within one poll, at the poll that holds the kernel lock while listing the stuck root; a write to a healthy file blocked at once, resolving every registered root; and the store builds before the first request, so a hung root at boot would stop the handshake (read in the code, not run). The server handles requests one at a time, so one stuck call also stops the rest. A model is not a hung drive: a real one may differ, and this cannot be staged safely on a drive in use. Measured for scale: 48 corpora and 72 roots build in 1.7 s in all, the slowest single corpus in 0.62 s.
- x-flag: LTS_CALL_WORKERS for slice 5 only (x-switch). With no slow root, replies carry nothing new, and a call during the read at boot waits as it does today ([MECH-BR-BOOT]).
- x-not-built: Everything in this file.
- x-authority: Requirement: an architects' ruling, not yet the maintainers': a bounded boot read is a precondition for a root on an external drive joining the start-up registry, never a release; such a root joins only after slice 5 (calls on workers; [PLAN-BR] x-order) has landed and the c10, c11 and c12 checks have run, and the overseeing architect says so. The maintainers have the finding in a report and have not ruled on it. The design, codes and numbers are the architects' too, except the boot speed limit in x-c8, which is the maintainers' ruling of 2026-11-11.
- x-precondition: The registry file and its pointer are outside this bound: they are read at boot and on every lts_list_corpora. They must not share a drive with a registered root that can hang. Stated, not enforced.

## Invariant: a hung root costs only what needs it [INV-BR-ONE-ROOT-ONE-COST]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: While one root is unresponsive, a read naming a corpus that has no root in common with it, a write to a file under no such root, and lts_list_corpora all answer within their normal time. A corpus-omitted search answers without the affected corpora and declares them. A corpus with several roots counts as affected if any root is marked, since a partial answer would read as whole.
- rationale: The defect is one disk taking every corpus with it.

## Invariant: not read yet is never empty [INV-BR-NOT-EMPTY]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: A corpus whose root is pending or unresponsive never answers with zero nodes, an empty digest or complete true. A call naming it is refused with a code. Its node count in a listing is null, never 0. A caller tells not read from empty by the status field, without trusting a count.
- rationale: A boot digest that reads as complete and empty is a loss nobody sees.

## Invariant: the write rules stay as they are [INV-BR-FAIL-CLOSED-KEPT]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: Nothing here relaxes the rule that a write refuses when the engine cannot prove where a file's history lives ([MECH-NR-LEDGER] x-legacy and x-state-declared). A root that does not answer is unknown, as a stat that fails with anything but not-found is there, and it refuses with the same code, state-unavailable (old-location-unknown); named-root-state-unprovable stays reserved for a failed registry entry. An unmounted root stays what it is today: not a failed entry. New write refusals join the ledger's stable codes and their fix text ([LEDGER-ONE-CURRENT-FILE]).

## Mechanism: one probe per root [MECH-BR-PROBE]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-states: Each registered root is in one state: ok, pending (never yet read), unresponsive (a probe overran its deadline). Missing and empty keep their existing meanings: a root that answers not-found is missing, not unresponsive.
- x-probe: All disk work for freshness on a root runs on that root's own worker, without holding the kernel lock. The lock guards only the in-memory results, and readers take the last result. A poll that finds a probe still running skips that root and, past the deadline, marks it unresponsive.
- x-no-pile-up: At most one job per root exists, ever: none starts while one is running, however long it runs. So a root hung for days costs one stuck thread, not one per poll. Jobs run on a small shared pool, so an idle server with many roots holds a few threads and not one per root, and a stuck job's thread is replaced, up to one per root marked. When the stuck call returns, the root is re-evaluated in full and goes back to ok, with no restart. The count of stuck workers is reported.
- x-deadlines: LTS_ROOT_DEADLINE_S, default 10, for one probe or one boot read of a root. Per root, with no total: a process naming several stalled corpora waits for each it needs, and a caller with its own limit plans for that. LTS_CALL_DEADLINE_S, default 30, for one tool call, above the 10 s per-file lock timeout so a busy file still gets its clean refusal. Both are the architects' judgement, the root's about 16 times the slowest whole corpus measured. Read once at process start. A root past the deadline is unresponsive until it answers; then it serves, with no restart.
- x-sites: The disk touches under a registered root, each of which moves to a probe's result or to a bounded worker: the kernel's listing and hashing, polled and verify-on-query; the gate's root-set check; root_health in every answer and in lts_list_corpora; membership's _norm and _contains; ledger.append's check_outside_roots; ledger.locate before and inside the lock; report_miss; lts_register_corpus, which hashes under the kernel lock; seat_store in the reviewer and the call CLI; the semantic index thread; membership's write guard for a corpus failed on an unknown key (the failed entry's path strings, added 2026-11-06 after the read of 49bfd12: an unbounded realpath there stalled every healthy write while the entry named a hung disk). A test plants a stalled call at each and shows the healthy corpus unaffected. A new touch site is a checklist finding, and so is a new CALLER of a helper in another module that touches the disk: the inventory reads call sites, so it cannot see one; the planted stall is the check.
- x-verify: The poll before a query is what keeps a write visible to the next read. So a named read of an ok root still verifies synchronously, on its own root's worker, up to the deadline; only other roots use last results. A write followed by a read returns the write ([MECH-BR-CALLS]), with a test.
- x-inventory: The list in x-sites is hand-made, so it is a starting point. Slice 1 adds a mechanical inventory: an AST scan of the engine and core packages for file-system calls (stat, realpath, open, scandir, listdir, walk, isdir, isfile, exists and their Path forms) that fails when a new one appears outside a declared list, each entry saying bounded, not under a root, or why not. Known misses to start from: the reviewer's corpora_containing, CLI writers, a profile read by lts_register_corpus or the library build_partition (the boot loader's profile read is bounded, slice 4), and _root_fingerprint. A second lts_register_corpus of a pending id refuses (slice 4). It is a tripwire, not enforcement.
- x-slow-not-hung: A slow root must not read as a hung one on a normal day's load, because a false mark at a render shows the failure banner on a healthy corpus. The deadline's default is a claim until measured: slice 1 records the machine load beside each scale figure and repeats them under the deployment's real load (a backup running, several servers re-executing after a land, a full suite). It reads an external drive's wake time before c10. If the numbers do not support 10 s, the default changes, or a render takes a longer deadline than a poll.
- x-progress: The deadline measures time with no progress, not elapsed time. A probe reports progress as each disk call returns, and it counts from the job's start, never from a waiter's queueing. So a large corpus hashing slowly under load is never marked, however long the pass takes, and a job stopped on one call is marked one deadline after it stopped. A waiter that gives up waiting marks nothing. A caller's wait for a slow root is its own to bound: the call deadline in slice 5. This is the answer to a slow root reading as hung; Where the resolve of a root's path cannot report progress per call (the fallback resolver, on an interpreter whose own realpath differs from the engine's copy), both the probe's resolve and the confirm use an ELAPSED BUDGET instead: the root deadline times the number of components of the path (counted lexically) plus a fixed margin of 1 second (FALLBACK_MARGIN_S), for that resolve only. A slow root of bounded depth whose every call answers is then neither marked nor refused, and a truly hung one still is, within that budget. Three limits of the fallback, never of the CPython copy (3.13.2): (a) with as many roots hung as the pool size, background polls can wait up to one fallback budget (root deadline times components plus 1 s) before the pool's cap grows; (b) the budget counts the declared spelling's components, so a root reached through a symlink to a deeper target may be refused membership-unconfirmed and its probe marked late though every call answers: fail closed, retryable, never a wrong attach. (c) Until slice 5 runs calls on workers, a first call that reaches a hung root not yet marked holds the single request thread for up to one fallback budget, not one root deadline (measured 2026-11-15 on 3.12: 13 s for a 12-component root at a 1 s deadline). The onset checks of x-first-hang run only on the copy; on the fallback, a fallback-mode test checks that the mark arrives within the budget. An operator sees which rules apply from the active resolver (lts_list_corpora carries realpath_impl: os.path.realpath only on the fallback; rootstate.REALPATH_IMPL always).  one read of one huge file on a slow disk is still a single call, so the default stays a judgement to measure.

## Mechanism: boot and build_store [MECH-BR-BOOT]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-server: The server reads roots on workers and answers the handshake without waiting for them. A corpus not yet read is pending and joins when its read ends. A call naming a pending corpus waits for it up to the root deadline, as a first call waits at boot today, then refuses root-unresponsive. A corpus-omitted read during that time waits the same way for an unread root, then leaves it out and declares it. So in the normal boot of about 1.6 s no call sees a refusal it would not see today. After a land every corpus is pending again, by the same rule. Registering a corpus id that is pending refuses (the loader is reading it), and unregistering one forgets it. A hop into ANY unserved corpus (pending; failed, with its own stable code, a failure because a root or a profile did not answer reading root-unresponsive; or with no roots, no-roots), also one that shares a domain suffix with a served corpus, is declared in routed_stale with that reason and unserved: true, as a hop into a served corpus whose root stopped answering is; a private one is the withheld stub.
- x-boot-window: A registry-error verdict that needs no disk (every entry-shape problem the shape phase can see) is reached in declare(), before any corpus is served, so no read or write sees a corpus the release would never serve. A registry-error verdict that is only known after a root has been read keeps x-server's boot window: until the first pass ends, the corpora already read are served (a named read answers, a write attributes by their roots), and registry-error mode begins when the first pass ends. A registry-error verdict met AFTER the first pass, by a corpus whose root did not answer at boot and is read on a retry, enters registry-error mode at that retry through the same step (seat the error, seal, empty), so the verdict is the release's and no corpus is ever in no table (added after the whole-slice read of 4384356, F1). Until that step, such a corpus stays pending (it is never dropped first) and is answered exactly as a pending corpus is: a call naming it waits up to one root deadline; if the step comes within the wait, the call meets registry-error mode's rules, and if not, it is refused corpus-verdict-pending, which states the true cause and never says a root did not answer. (Added after the reads of 5f4b595, fd47c36 and 34e9668.) The window lasts at most the first pass, so it grows with the corpora read and the machine's load (measured: 0.15 to 0.23 s beside one 4,000-node corpus, load not recorded; 2.4 to 2.7 s beside one 12,000-node corpus at load 10 to 24, the read of 60410b8; up to the root deadline with a hung root, read from the code, not run); receipt and ledger agree across the change (101 writes over 30 boots). Ruled 2026-11-06 after the read of fbb55c2, P2-1.
- x-load-error: An unexpected error while loading one corpus fails that corpus only. This covers anything other than a root or profile that did not answer, or a corpus-file failure, both of which already fail their own corpus. It goes to failed_corpora in one store step with code corpus-load-error, path null and the error's class name, and no message or path in any reply. This holds on the first pass and after it: a retry load that raises never leaves its corpus in no table. The server never enters registry-error mode for it; that verdict stays for a registry that cannot be read and a store with no corpus loadable. ENTRY SHAPE (third amendment, 2026-11-06, after the read of fe465bc; it replaces the shape rulings of 37ec281 and 2359cc4, each of which moved a verdict the maintainers own): this spec changes no entry shape's BOOT verdict. Each malformed or unusual shape (a string or int `roots`, a non-object entry, a blank or missing path, a non-boolean `private`, `roots: null`, and the rest) fails the whole registry, fails only its own corpus, or serves, exactly as release af4cfc2 does, pinned by one table test over every shape that compares the branch with af4cfc2 THROUGH THE REAL SERVER'S BOOT (a stdio server with its kernel, loader and first pass, read through lts_list_corpora and a named call), never through a library function the server does not use; the table's honesty check (the release answers the pinned file) runs in the gate, not only in a git clone. Changing a shape's verdict is the maintainers' call, not this spec's. What this spec adds is on the write side and the disk side only. (1) A write is never ATTRIBUTED by a location the registry cannot state. Precisely (clarified 2026-11-06 after the read of 90737fb): an entry the release SERVES has a stated location, its declared roots, and is never treated as unstatable, whatever the shape judge says of its other fields (a non-boolean private or an int domain_suffix still has readable roots). An entry the release does NOT serve whose roots cannot be read as paths, or whose root names a named root the table does not hold (named-root-unknown, named-roots-malformed: a typo'd ${...} reference, added 2026-11-06 after the whole-slice read of 4384356, F2; realistic, as registry edits are), (a registry-error shape, or a key-failed entry with a root-ish string that is not path-like, such as a bare relative word) has no stated location. While such an entry stands, every write lands flagged (corpora_error, corpora null). Flagging is not refusing: writes stay available. This is the SECOND deliberate difference from the release, on the write side only (corrected 2026-11-06 after the read of fbb55c2: the earlier text said the release already flags these writes, an unmeasured premise; at af4cfc2 a write under the healthy corpus beside a registry-error shape such as path null lands ATTRIBUTED to that corpus). The branch flags it because the registry holds an entry whose location cannot be read, so no attribution can be proved. The verdict table records each such shape's write as an expected difference, by name. No write is called outside every root because of it. The flag clears as follows, by route (measured 2026-11-07, pinned by tests). The route is keyed on where the flag comes from. (1) An entry the store records as unlocated (a failed entry whose location the registry cannot state: a bare word in a mistyped key such as `pth: "bad"`, or a malformed location shape) is cleared by `lts_unregister_corpus` (success, was_unlocated) and by fixing the entry and registering it. (2) An entry holding a `${...}` reference (in any key, a mistyped one such as `pth: "${nosuch}/thing"` included) or failing on a named-root error (`named-root-unknown`, `named-roots-malformed`) is flagged by the guard from the registry itself, not by the store: `lts_unregister_corpus` answers `no such loaded corpus` and does not clear it, and the flag clears when the registry file is fixed and the entry is registered again (`lts_register_corpus`), or at a restart. (2) A failed entry's path-like strings are UNTRUSTED ROOTS and are handled by the machinery for declared roots ([MECH-BR-PROBE], [MECH-BR-WRITES]), never by a disk call on the writer's thread. Each string has a probe that keeps its real path. A write is a candidate when its file lies under the string as typed or under the kept real path. A candidate lands flagged and unattributed, never refused for being a candidate. A string that is pending or unresponsive is counted in membership_partial's marked_count. A write whose file lies under it AS TYPED is refused root-unresponsive before anything is read, as for a marked declared root, and every other write is unaffected ([INV-BR-ONE-ROOT-ONE-COST]). A non-boolean `private` counts as private on every surface that counts or names corpora, whatever its boot verdict. That is the one DELIBERATE difference from the release, and it changes how a corpus is named, never whether it serves or fails: where the release names such a corpus (in failed_corpora, a listing, an error), the branch counts it in private_failed instead (corrected 2026-11-06 after the read of 594ef8a: the earlier text said it changes no verdict, which was wider than the code). The verdict table records each such row as an expected difference, by name, so no other difference can hide among them. The rules hold on every route a write takes (server, call CLI, library writers with no store) and in lts_register_corpus, which runs the same shape check and refuses with a code, never raw error text. A blocking library caller (config.build_store) gets the same failed_corpora entry, not an exception, and each in-process caller's owner is told of the new code before a land ([INPROCESS-BUILD-STORE-CONTRACT]). Rationale: one corpus's unexpected exception took a healthy, served corpus with it, and the reply blamed the registry ([INV-BR-ONE-ROOT-ONE-COST]). The architects' ruling, 2026-11-06, from the read of 5d668c4 (P2-3, P2-4).
- x-library: config.build_store, used by any process that builds without a server, takes the bound as well: a corpus whose read overruns goes into failed_corpora with code corpus-root-unresponsive and is not served. find on it returns success false without touching the disk, and self_heal cannot re-enter a read of it, with a test. A process-wide cap holds at most one stuck worker per root and 16 in all: past that, or for a root with a worker still stuck, build_store marks the corpus failed at once with no new thread (with 16 stuck workers held, a corpus on a healthy root with no worker is failed too, and clears when the workers return), so a caller building repeatedly, per request, cannot pile threads up. Stuck workers are daemon threads, and a short-lived process exits at once.
- x-render: A consumer that renders agent definitions builds its own store for each corpus it names, in its own process, so this bound is the only one it gets. The refusal reaches its existing failure path and shows as its expansion-failed banner, also under empty=ok, never as an empty digest. A failed corpus needs its own branch in the engine's failed-view reply, which assumes a file path and says to fix that file; a hung root has neither. Other in-process callers of build_store get the bound unasked, and their owner checks each fails loud: a saved decision answer above all. The stall harness is an importable module that a consumer's suite can use.
- x-built-slice4: One loader (factlattice_engine/bootload.py) is both config.build_store (blocking: up to 4 loader threads, every root read at once as a job on the process registry, a corpus whose root does not answer goes to failed_corpora as corpus-root-unresponsive with path null, a root with a stuck worker or a process at 16 stuck fails its corpus at once with no thread) and the server's boot (a store of pending corpora, a loader in the background, each corpus published in one step under the store's condition, a stuck corpus kept pending and loaded again by the watcher cycle once its root is ok). The server's first pass starts one loader and adds one, up to four, for each corpus read that runs GROW_AFTER_S without finishing; the blocking build_store starts four at once. With K roots that do not answer, healthy corpora are served after about GROW_AFTER_S per stuck read while K is below four, and after ceil(K/4) root deadlines from then on, as before this rule. Each live root's read, hash and observation run as one job on that root's worker (the seat uses its prepared state; a declared root that is not live still gets a seat job); the publish into the store stays one locked step. (Added after the read of 88c8f3b, for the boot speed rule c8.) A corpus read is split so the disk half (CorpusIndex.read_root) runs on the root's own worker and the merge (finish_cold_build, config.partition_from_build) does not. The corpus's profile file is read like a root read (a job on the process registry, keyed by the profile path), so a profile under a hung root fails its own corpus; the blocking caller's wait for the loader threads ends one deadline after the last sign of life. Not bounded, named: lts_register_corpus on an engine with no kernel. Read once per process now: LTS_ROOT_DEADLINE_S. The ledger table is the booted one from the first instant (a provisional pin with the roots' lexical spellings, replaced by the final pin when the declared roots' real paths have been resolved, bounded); that late resolve may follow a link that was swapped during a stall, which is the probe's concern, not the table's, and is not changed. A corpus whose profile does not answer is failed corpus-root-unresponsive with reason profile after one deadline, in the server as in a library, and the watcher reads it again when the profile answers. A hop into any unserved corpus (pending, or failed for any code, or with no roots), including one that shares a domain suffix with a served corpus, is declared stale with that corpus's reason. The ledger table pin moved after the first pass, bounded and retried per watcher cycle, because resolving every declared root can stop on a hung one; the table itself is built at boot from the registry the store was built from (ledger.context_deferred), never from a second read. The registry-error verdict for nothing loadable reaches the long-running server when the first pass ends, not before the handshake. The store's four tables (served, pending, failed, no roots) follow one rule: code outside the store that reads more than one of them takes them from one snapshot under the store's lock and reads no table itself, and a move between tables is one store method whose precondition is checked inside that same locked step (a register that built while the watcher moved the corpus to pending refuses at the seat and discards its build). Since round 7 the tables are private to the store: outside code gets them only from its snapshot methods, a call takes one look at each decision point and hands it to its helpers, and a test lists every function that takes a look and every site where a helper still takes its own (the smaller form).

## Mechanism: calls [MECH-BR-CALLS]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-gate: The gate reads a corpus's freshness from its roots' last results and never touches the disk for a root marked unresponsive. A call naming a marked corpus is refused root-unresponsive with what clears it.
- x-first-hang: A call can reach a root before any poll has marked it. The server therefore runs each tool call on a worker and waits up to the call deadline. Workers never touch stdin or stdout; the main thread writes each reply and holds SERVING_LOCK across the wait, as the stdin carry needs. On overrun it replies call-overran, marks the roots the call named, and takes the next request. For a writer, call-overran means outcome unknown, as create_outcome_unknown does: re-read before retrying, since the abandoned call may still finish. Lint, self-check and other compute-bound tools are held to the same deadline only where they touch a root; a tool may declare a longer one. The cap: at most 8 abandoned calls per root named, and 16 in all, counting calls with no root, such as a write to an arbitrary path; past the first only that root's calls refuse engine-saturated, past the second all do. Loud, and it clears as they return. The case slice 5 has to close, measured on slice 2 (a real server, a model of a hang): a call that reaches a hung root that is not yet marked holds the single request thread for one deadline, whether it NAMES the root or is corpus-omitted (an unnamed find probes every root first: 0.43 s on a 0.4 s deadline), and a healthy read or listing queued behind it waits as long (2.71 s behind a named read on a 3 s deadline), once per hang onset. At the default 10 s the wait would be about 10 s: an extrapolation, not run. Slice 2's marks bound everything after the onset, not the onset; tests/test_bounded_roots_probes.py keeps a strict red record of it.
- x-order: Replies stay in request order and calls stay one at a time, apart from abandoned ones. A write abandoned while it holds a per-file lock keeps that lock until it returns; a later write to that file meets the lock's existing timeout and refusal.
- x-reexec: os.execv destroys abandoned threads rather than draining them, and a write cut off between its file replace and its ledger append leaves no record. So the build-stamp sentinel defers a re-exec while any abandoned call is a write, reports it in lts_list_corpora, and re-execs when none remains. A land during a long stall therefore doesn't reach that server until the stall clears or the session restarts, and it says so. Abandoned reads do not defer it. Whether exec can itself be held by a thread stuck on a hung mount is unmeasured; the land checks read every server after a land anyway, and that is what would catch it.
- x-overran-text: call-overran on a writer carries in its reply and evidence: outcome_unknown true, and the step, read the file or lts_history before any retry. The call's roots are marked, so a blind retry naming them refuses root-unresponsive at once, writing nothing. A write to a path under no registered root has no mark, and a retry there meets the writers' own gate, which refuses an exact repeat of a node as duplicate_node_id. A test retries blindly and shows no double write.
- x-reexec-rollback: A rollback must not wait on the defect it rolls back. So a deferred re-exec is bounded: after LTS_REEXEC_DEFER_S, default 900, the server re-execs anyway and records the abandoned write as possibly unledgered, in the state folder, for the next lts_history to report. A land record's rollback step also says: a server that still shows reexec_deferred is restarted by its session, and is read afterwards.
- x-switch: Slice 5 changes how every call runs in every session, so it has a switch: LTS_CALL_WORKERS, default off when it lands, returning calls to the request thread. Turned on one session at a time after a soak, on evidence. With it off, the first-hang case is not bounded and a first stuck call stops that server, as today; the marks from probes still bound everything after it. The flag is removed once it has run through a stall on real load.
- x-mark-clears: A mark set by call-overran clears only when the abandoned call returns, or a probe begun after it succeeds. So while a mark stands, later calls naming that root refuse at once and add nothing to the cap. The per-root cap counts calls still abandoned after their root has cleared; the overall cap also counts calls with no root.
- x-boundary-whole-call: The worker boundary is the whole tool call at tools.dispatch, querylog.record_call included, not _dispatch alone. Measured at engine 8c0eb80 with tests/stall_harness.py, deadline 3 s, a model of a hang and no real hung drive (the request-thread measurement of 2026-11-12 at 8c0eb80): one add_property to a file under a just-stalled root made 79 request-thread disk calls at 34 sites (sections 4a, 5). Fixing one site moves the block to the next: with only isfile, only os.open or only scandir stalled, each blocked the write on its own, at least 12 s on a 3 s deadline (section 6). The log write is in dispatch, outside _dispatch (tools.py:3964 at 8c0eb80), and with the state dir stalled it held every tool, healthy corpora included (section 4c row 37, staged in section 6). Per-site bounding was measured to move the block, not remove it, so the boundary is the call, not a list of sites (the architects' agreement of 2026-11-12/13). Checked by x-c13: request_thread_hits empty for every scenario, the state-dir stall among them.
- x-path-tools-marked: lts_lint (path=, paths= and corpus=), lts_property_loss (roots= and path=), lts_preview and lts_find with path= refuse root-unresponsive when the path resolves under a marked root, as the typed writers already do (authoring.marked_refusal): one rule for every path-taking tool. Today they have no probe and no gate (report rows 23 to 26). Staged at 8c0eb80 with the root stalled and not marked, each blocked at least 12 s on a 3 s deadline: lts_find path=, lts_lint path= on a root and on a file, lts_property_loss roots=, lts_preview corpus= (its path= form read only) (section 6). That they block as well when the root is marked is read from the code, not staged: they never ask a probe (section 5). The roots such a call names for the call-overran mark are derived from its path, not from a corpus argument. lts_preview corpus=<id> naming a marked corpus refuses root-unresponsive as x-gate requires of every named call; today it has no gate (row 26). Checked by x-c13: the four path-tool scenarios with the root not marked, and the same four, plus lts_preview corpus=, with the root marked first, each refusing root-unresponsive with no disk call under that root. The refusal is the one x-gate already gives a named read, in the same reply shape (success false, reason root-unresponsive, error_class RootUnresponsive, with what clears it, under x-c7's privacy rule), so one code has one shape. It never carries has_errors, a findings list or a count, so a machine tells it from a finding. A caller reads it as "could not run" (its result unvalidated), never as "found errors".
- x-state-dir-bound: The engine's state dir gets its own bound. A call whose only slow disk is the state dir replies call-overran within the call deadline (no root deadline applies, since no root is named), and counts under calls with no root in the cap (x-first-hang). Measured at 8c0eb80 with the state dir stalled: lts_find, lts_list_corpora, lts_find_in_prose, lts_history, lts_report_miss, and add_property to a file outside every root each blocked at least 12 s on a 3 s deadline (report section 6; rows 37 to 41). Until slice 5 lands the standing stop stays: the state dir is on the internal disk, checked by a land-step check at every land. Checked by x-c13 (the state-dir stall) and x-c5 (the cap).
- x-reverse-onset: lts_reverse naming a healthy corpus does not pay a root deadline because another root is hung. Today it waits for every corpus's probe: 3.03 s on a 3 s deadline with another root stalled and not yet marked (report section 4d; tools.py:1611 at 8c0eb80), so [INV-BR-ONE-ROOT-ONE-COST] does not hold for it at onset. It gets the treatment find got: the named corpus verifies, other roots use their last results ([MECH-BR-PROBE] x-verify). Checked by x-c2's reverse on the healthy corpus, with an onset case: the other root stalled and not yet marked. An answer that leaves a corpus unverified names it as an unnamed find does: each such corpus in extent.corpora_skipped as {corpus, reason}, with the reason the find path gives it (x-codes: root-pending, root-unresponsive, corpus-verdict-pending, corpus-root-unresponsive), and a private one counted, not named (x-c7). Top-level complete is false whenever reverse already sets it false (any stale corpus) and also for each of those not-read reasons; this never makes reverse's complete true where it is false today. Consumers rule on complete and corpora_skipped; reverse's existing stale_corpora and private_stale_count stay and are not the rule. A root not yet marked is answered on its last result, as find's is, so during a hang's onset an answer can miss a referrer written since that result while complete reads true: the same window find has. A consumer reads complete false as "unknown", never as "no referrer": a supersession or other referrer may sit in the unverified corpus.
- x-open-unstaged-calls: Open, not decided. What the stall harness cannot block stays unmeasured: os.DirEntry methods, os.readlink, os.chmod, os.chown, shutil.copystat, the xattr copy through libc.copyfile (edit.py:506 at 8c0eb80) and calls inside C extensions, so the write's replace and create steps (report rows 18 and 21) have sites it cannot show (report section 7). A real hung drive is not run.
- x-open-post-verify-windows: Open, not decided. The reads after a bounded verify are unbounded windows until an engine hook can stage them: the body index (lts_find_in_prose), the source excerpt (lts_node), lts_history, validate_source_file (lts_find source_file=) and the heal rebuild (report rows 29 to 33, read from the code, not run). A stall that begins between the probe and the end of the read is not bounded; for the body index and a full heal that window is as long as reading the whole corpus. This harness cannot stage one: its stall is on or off for the whole run, so the probe marks the root first (section 6).
- x-measurement-cited: Where the properties of this node and of [ACC-BR] added for slice 5 say "report", "section N" or "row N", they mean the request-thread measurement of 2026-11-12 at 8c0eb80. It is held by the architects outside this repository, so a reader of this file cannot open it; the numbers it gives are stated in the properties themselves, and its sections and rows are cited for the maintainers, who hold it.

## Mechanism: writes and membership [MECH-BR-WRITES]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-membership: Each root's real path is resolved by its probe and kept with its result. The kept path is a fact about the last answer: a link swapped while a server runs reads the old place until the next probe, one poll later. (A) Membership is evaluated ONCE per write, at its start: one result, kept for the call, and the ledger record, the edge context and the receipt all read that one result, so they always agree. (B) Kept paths decide the refusal (a file under a marked root, matched case-insensitively and with Unicode composition folded on every volume, fail closed) and which roots are CANDIDATES (a root whose kept real path holds the file's real path, or whose declared spelling holds the path as typed). The ATTACH decision confirms each ok candidate with one fresh realpath of that root, resolved with CPython's own realpath algorithm plus a progress report after each disk call (when the running interpreter's posixpath.realpath equals the copy, checked at import and falling back on any failure of the comparison; otherwise os.path.realpath under the elapsed budget of x-progress; lts_list_corpora then carries realpath_impl: os.path.realpath), so a slow root whose every call answers in time is never late. A write attaches to a corpus only if the file's real path lies under that fresh path, so a stale kept path or a link inside a root never attaches it to the wrong corpus. (C) The confirm runs on an auxiliary slot of its own, foreground, never queued behind the root's probe work; AT MOST ONE confirm per root is in flight. A write may use a confirm's answer only if that confirm job STARTED after the write's own file resolve returned: a write that finds one running that began earlier, or one that finished before it began, waits for it to end and then starts or joins one that began after (confirms for one root serialise); a write that finds one that began after its resolve JOINS it and waits for its result. No second thread starts. The wait is bounded by no-progress time per disk call (the confirm reports progress after each component of the path), so the total wait for one confirm is at most the number of its calls times the root deadline, not the root deadline; a write can wait for TWO confirms (a stale one, then its own), so at most twice that. (D) A confirm NEVER marks a root: only a root's probes mark it, and the root stays ok. If the confirm for ANY candidate root that could hold THIS file, by its kept real path or by its declared spelling however the file was typed, does not answer, that write is refused membership-unconfirmed (retryable, nothing written; the evaluation is never repeated within the write); writes under other roots are unaffected, and the slot recovers when the stuck call returns. When the registry itself cannot be read (a RegistryError, or a failure inside the roots lookup) the pinned rule applies instead: that failure is the one result of the write's membership evaluation, kept for the whole write, so nothing evaluates membership again in the same write; the write lands under today's key, and the record is flagged (corpora_error), with no membership_partial at all; any other failure to evaluate membership refuses membership-unconfirmed. (E) A write resolves a root in exactly one case: this confirm. A marked or pending root is never resolved. The bounded reads of a process with a kernel run on the kernel's registry, so a root has one running job and stuck_workers is the true count. The claim x-c9 makes is no wrong record: a write may be refused membership-unconfirmed, and may carry membership_partial, but is never attached to the wrong corpus or silently partial. The purpose is kept: no write waits without limit on a root's disk. An earlier change that cached a disk fact for speed regressed; so a test swaps a link and shows the next poll sees it.
- x-healthy-file: A write to a file under no marked root proceeds as today, with its gate, lock and ledger. The membership result may be partial because a registered root is pending or unresponsive. Then the receipt and the ledger record carry membership_partial: {marked_count, unconfirmed_count} (marked_count counts roots that are pending or unresponsive and nothing else; unconfirmed_count counts candidate roots whose confirm did not answer, which is 0 on every write that lands (on the registry-stage path membership is not evaluated at all and corpora_error is the signal), since a candidate root (one that could hold the file, by its kept real path or its declared spelling, however the file was typed) whose confirm does not answer refuses it membership-unconfirmed), name no corpus, and the receipt does NOT say the file is outside every root (inside_corpus is null, no warning). A partial result makes the edge context incomplete: a change that could be an edge key refuses edge-context-incomplete unless allow_edge_change, as for a profile that cannot be determined. A named-roots table adds one case: if a marked root is one the resolver would need to decide whether a ledger under an old spelling belongs to this file, the write is refused state-unavailable (old-location-unknown), by the rule that history must be provable. Otherwise it proceeds. Writers in other products are told of the new refusals (root-unresponsive, membership-unconfirmed) and of call-overran before a land, and the checklist of whoever owns one gets a rule.
- x-inside-marked: A write to a file under a marked root is refused root-unresponsive before anything is read, and nothing is written. A file inside no registered root is the caller's own path: it meets only the call deadline.

## Mechanism: what is reported [MECH-BR-REPORT]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-list: lts_list_corpora never waits on a marked root. An affected entry carries fresh false, total_nodes and total_nodes_live null, not 0, and root_health {status: pending | unresponsive, roots: count, since}, extending the existing shape; a corpus with an empty root keeps today's total of 0 and its empty_roots, so status tells them apart with no count trusted. extent.notes never lists a marked root by absolute path unless it is public. A private corpus is counted as today, also while pending, never named. The reply adds stuck_workers, abandoned_calls and reexec_deferred when above zero.
- x-extent: A corpus-omitted search lists affected public corpora in corpora_skipped with reason root-pending or root-unresponsive, one anonymous count for private ones, complete false. With a default scope, stale_count counts them.
- x-codes: Stable and pinned by a test: root-unresponsive, call-overran, engine-saturated, corpus-root-unresponsive, corpus-load-error, corpus-entry-shape (lts_register_corpus's refusal of an entry whose shape the schema does not accept), corpus-verdict-pending (a call naming a corpus held pending for the registry-error verdict, still held when its one-root-deadline wait ended; also the corpora_skipped reason for such a corpus on a corpus-omitted read, and a write refusal; retryable, never says a root did not answer). root_health.status pending is a state in a listing, not a refusal code: a call naming a pending corpus waits, by x-server; a corpus held for the verdict also carries root_health.reason verdict-pending (additive). Each refusal says what clears it. No reply gives an absolute path for a private root.

## Acceptance [ACC-BR]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-instrument: A stall harness in the suite, importable by a consumer's suite: a real server process started through a test launcher that installs the block and then runs the server module, with no engine seam and no variable a server reads. It confirms the block is on, or raises rather than reading as a red result, and each test asserts a hit or states why none is expected. It lists the calls it blocks (stat, lstat, scandir, listdir, walk, open, os.open, os.replace, realpath, isdir, isfile, exists, link, mkdir, rmdir, access, fsync, unlink and Path forms), with a planted site for each shown red, and states what it cannot block, such as DirEntry methods and calls on an open descriptor. It models a hang, stated in every result, as is that no real hung drive was run.
- x-red-first: Every check below is first shown failing on the prior commit for the reason it names; the stall-one record at 19d6511 is the baseline.
- x-c1: Hung at boot: handshake under 1 s; the other corpus answers named and unnamed; a call naming the hung one waits up to the root deadline, then refuses root-unresponsive; its listing shows pending, then unresponsive, with null totals.
- x-c2: Hung after boot: named read, prose, node, reverse and list on the healthy corpus within normal time for 60 s, through many polls; unnamed search declares the omission with complete false. A write to a healthy file then a named read returns the write. ROOT-SET-CHANGE still reads a changed root set as not fresh.
- x-c3: Writes: a healthy file writes with gate and ledger whole and membership_partial, past all of membership, check_outside_roots and locate; a file in the marked root refuses before any read, bytes unchanged; a first-hang write replies call-overran, and when released, the outcome is consistent with the ledger and the file. A re-exec requested meanwhile waits for it: reexec_deferred shows, and it runs after release.
- x-c4: Release: when the stall ends the root returns to ok with no restart, the corpus serves fresh, and no record is lost.
- x-c5: No pile-up: hang one root across 200 polls: the engine's thread count rises by at most one for it, counting the engine's threads and not the test's. Abandon calls with no root until 16 reply call-overran: the next refuses engine-saturated, and it clears on release. A root's mark standing keeps later calls from adding to the cap.
- x-c6: build_store with a hung root returns within the deadline, with the code in failed_corpora and the process able to exit at once. find on it returns success false with no disk call, including through self_heal. Repeated build_store calls during the stall add no threads past the cap. A consumer's rendered marker for that corpus shows its failure banner, with and without empty=ok.
- x-c7: Privacy: a private root hung: no id or path in any reply, error or log. The privacy tripwire test passes with the new touch sites declared.
- x-c8: No slower when healthy: boot, named find and write against the prior commit, on all registered corpora, with the load stated. Boot is measured by the paired study in tools/bounded_reads_bench.py, which runs identical code and a planted slowdown through the same machinery. The limit is at most 12% on boot p50 against the prior commit, measured on the real start-up registry, met only when the whole interval is under it, and only in a run where identical code stays within a narrow band and a planted 10% is detected; any other result is undecidable, not a pass. (The maintainers' ruling of 2026-11-11. The architects' first limit, 5% on a synthetic model, was not met: three runs on the real registry gave 1.082, 1.084 and 1.096, with intervals together 1.073 to 1.103, and the synthetic model 1.104. 12% sits just above the worst interval measured, so this slice passes it by construction; its job from here is to catch a later regression.) A named find is measured after every server is ready (every corpus read), by the paired study's find mode with the same controls; a find during the first pass has no prior-commit equivalent and is reported with no limit. Boot p95 is reported with its interval and carries no limit: on one deployment at load 13 to 18, identical code gave p95 intervals as wide as 0.066 to 1.695, so a limit there cannot be tested. A named find is at most 2 ms slower at p95. lts_list_corpora is reported with no limit. The boot limit is the maintainers' ruling; the find limit and the reporting rules are the architects' judgement.
- x-c9: Link swap: a root's link changed while serving is seen within one poll, and a write in between lands by its own real path with no wrong record.
- x-c10: Fresh server on a real external drive, present and absent, started as sessions start theirs: no prompt, no hang, other corpora unaffected. When the drive's owner allows it. This does not cover a hang and cannot.
- x-falsifies: If a real stall is ever observed that the harness did not model, or c8 cannot be met, the design goes back to the maintainers. (c8 at the architects' 5% could not be met; it went to them, and they ruled the 12% limit on 2026-11-11, accepting about 0.15 to 0.17 s per engine start for a stuck root costing only its own corpora.)
- x-c11: Under the deployment's real load, as x-slow-not-hung names: no root marked and no render shows a banner, across a full boot of every server and repeated renders. The load is recorded with the result. This is the check for a false mark, as c8 is for slowness.
- x-c12: Render time with every root of a definition's corpora stalled, from the render's own process: it stays inside the launcher's stated limit for its hook, or the limit is broken on the record and goes to that consumer's owner. Stated per definition, not per deployment.
- x-c13: Calls on workers, with LTS_CALL_WORKERS on (with it off the same scenarios stay red, as x-switch says), by the verification step of the request-thread measurement of 2026-11-12 at 8c0eb80, section 9: tests/stall_harness.py, deadline 3 s, no settle, repeated at two loads with uptime recorded. Every scenario in the 12 rows of section 6's table (the last row is the state-dir stall; some rows hold several calls), and the marked-root cases x-path-tools-marked names. Pass: every call replies call-overran or root-unresponsive within the root deadline plus the call deadline (the call deadline alone for the state-dir stall, x-state-dir-bound); a healthy call sent 0.3 s after is answered in under 1 s; request_thread_hits(srv) (tests/test_bounded_roots_probes.py) is empty for every scenario. Each scenario is first shown red on the prior commit: the report is that record for 8c0eb80 (no reply at 12 s, and the healthy call waits too), and it is re-run on the slice's base. The hit list is the check for a new site: the inventory of x-inventory cannot see a new caller (rows 12, 20 and 28 were reached through callers in other modules). Each refusal and each incomplete answer is asserted field by field, as x-path-tools-marked and x-reverse-onset state them.
- x-c14: With LTS_CALL_WORKERS on, a write abandoned after its file replace and before its ledger append, under the same stall: reexec_deferred shows in lts_list_corpora (x-reexec), and once LTS_REEXEC_DEFER_S has passed (the test sets it to a few seconds), the x-reexec-rollback record names the write as possibly unledgered for the next lts_history (the request-thread measurement of 2026-11-12 at 8c0eb80, section 9, item 4).

## Build order and rollout [PLAN-BR]

- part-of: [FL-BOUNDED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-order: Slice 1, the harness and red records. Slice 2, probes off the lock, gate and report. Slice 3, membership and write rules. Slice 4, boot and build_store. Slice 5, calls on workers and the re-exec rule, the widest change, last. A cold read on each. A root under an external drive joins the start-up registry only after slice 5, c10, c11 and c12 (c11 and c12 added by the overseeing architect's ruling of 2026-11-05), and by a further word from the overseeing architect: passing them is necessary, not a release.
- x-rollback: Engine rollback only; no data format changes.
- x-extension-points: SDLC 7.5 and 7.6 gain: [ROOT-READS-BOUNDED] (no code path resolves, lists or opens under a registered root while holding the kernel lock, or from the request thread without a deadline, and a test plants one); [ROOT-STATE-ONE-REGISTRY] (ok, pending and unresponsive live in one place, and a new state reports through it); [CALL-WORKER-NO-STDIO] (workers never touch stdin or stdout; the sentinel defers on an abandoned write); [INPROCESS-BUILD-STORE-CONTRACT] (a library caller gets failed_corpora codes and is told of a new one). [STORE-TABLES-ONE-SNAPSHOT-ONE-STEP] (reads of several tables are one snapshot, moves check their precondition in the locked step; a scan with a planted control for every pair of the four tables). STDIN-CARRY, ROOT-SET-CHANGE, DECLARED-ROOT-HEALTH and the report_miss root guard are updated. SPEC.md states the states, codes and fields with an absence test. The five new codes are pinned. New codes that refuse a write join the ledger's list. Every new corpus-collection touch joins FANOUT_ALLOWLIST. A launcher or writer owner's rule goes to their own checklist.
- x-build-traps: Found by reading the code against this spec; each needs a test before its slice is called done. (1) A store with every corpus pending is empty, so boot must not take the no-corpus-loaded registry-error path, the profile audit or seat_store as they stand. (2) A process with no probe (the call CLI, the reviewer, an in-process writer) has no kept real paths, and membership falls back to resolving declared roots: it needs its own bounded resolve; a pending or failed corpus must not make a write under its root read as outside every root; and state roots and old-spelling stats need the bound too. (3) The code-currency sentinel stops after its first firing, so deferring a re-exec means it must keep running and re-check; which tools count as a write for this is one stated list. (4) A named read that joins a probe already running may see pre-write content, so a read after a write starts from a result begun after that write. (5) lts_list_corpora never waits, including during boot, where it reports pending; a write during the boot window waits as a named read does, and no-corpus tools do not wait. A call's per-root waits are bounded by the call deadline. Slice 3 refused a write under a pending root as root-unresponsive (fail closed); slice 4 changed that refusal into a wait (membership.await_pending, run ahead of the refusal in both write doors): the write waits up to the root deadline for the pending root's first read, takes a new reading of the roots, and then lands or is refused root-unresponsive by the ordinary rule, with no disk touched on the writing thread. (6) The named-call refusal path assumes a failed corpus has a file path, as the failed view does. (7) The test marker for the stall hook is defined in slice 1, and a shipped server's refusal to honour it has a test.
- x-prelands: Named steps before a land, each with an owner who has said yes, not merely been told. Slice 4: the renderer's owner shows a stalled decision-answer root fails its save loudly, and adds the banner test. Slice 5: under LTS_CALL_WORKERS off; a land record says why it is safe to land with nobody at the keyboard, or it waits for the maintainers' return. Each writer owner's rule goes on its own checklist.
- x-slice5-scope: Slice 5 in x-order includes [MECH-BR-CALLS] x-boundary-whole-call, x-path-tools-marked, x-state-dir-bound and x-reverse-onset, and [ACC-BR] x-c13 and x-c14 (added 2026-11-15 from the request-thread measurement of 2026-11-12 at 8c0eb80). A root on an external drive therefore waits for them too, by x-order.
